WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:31.002 --> 00:00:32.790
- Hi everyone, Sal Khan
here from Khan Academy.

00:00:32.790 --> 00:00:35.450
We wanna welcome you to
our daily live stream.

00:00:35.450 --> 00:00:37.680
This is just a way for us to stay in touch

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:39.950
and have interesting conversations.

00:00:39.950 --> 00:00:42.220
We started during times of school closures

00:00:42.220 --> 00:00:43.760
'cause obviously we're
all social distanced

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:46.140
and it's that much more
important to stay in touch,

00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:48.800
but we think it'll be fun,
even potentially beyond

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:51.310
if we, hopefully we
get through this period

00:00:51.310 --> 00:00:52.350
sooner then later.

00:00:52.350 --> 00:00:55.790
I do remind everyone that
we are a not-for-profit

00:00:55.790 --> 00:00:58.830
and the only way we're
able to do all of what do,

00:00:58.830 --> 00:00:59.880
and especially all of the things

00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:02.300
that we're trying to
around COVID-19 support,

00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:05.230
is through philanthropic donations
from folks like yourself.

00:01:05.230 --> 00:01:07.210
I do wanna give a special shout-out

00:01:07.210 --> 00:01:10.090
to several of corporations
who've stepped up,

00:01:10.090 --> 00:01:12.680
especially since the
crisis, Bank of America,

00:01:12.680 --> 00:01:17.680
Goggle.org, AT&amp;T, Fastly
and Novartis for stepping up

00:01:18.083 --> 00:01:19.920
and the many other supporters before that.

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:22.107
We can't do this work,
many of y'all might wonder,

00:01:22.107 --> 00:01:24.460
"how is this free," it's because of

00:01:24.460 --> 00:01:26.300
those philanthropic
donations and Khan Academy

00:01:26.300 --> 00:01:29.750
is much more than myself,
it's over 200 full-time folks

00:01:29.750 --> 00:01:33.600
working around the world and
obviously we need resources

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:35.210
in order to do this work.

00:01:35.210 --> 00:01:37.750
So I'm super excited about today's guest.

00:01:37.750 --> 00:01:39.560
I have Kellye Testy

00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:42.460
from the Law School
Admission Council joining us

00:01:42.460 --> 00:01:45.020
and Kellye good to see you.

00:01:45.020 --> 00:01:47.810
- It's great to see you and
so great to be with you.

00:01:47.810 --> 00:01:49.263
We love the Khan Academy.

00:01:50.652 --> 00:01:53.480
- And we love the Law
School Admission's Council.

00:01:53.480 --> 00:01:55.057
So we'll talk a little bit
about our relationship,

00:01:55.057 --> 00:01:57.760
but I'll remind everyone to go on to,

00:01:57.760 --> 00:02:00.460
whether you're watching this
on YouTube, or Facebook,

00:02:00.460 --> 00:02:03.570
and go on the message boards,
ask any questions you'd like

00:02:03.570 --> 00:02:07.590
of Kellye and myself and
Kellye is expert on all things

00:02:07.590 --> 00:02:11.420
law and law school, so I don't think

00:02:11.420 --> 00:02:13.080
we're actually gonna
be giving legal advice,

00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:16.420
but careers (laughing) careers in law,

00:02:16.420 --> 00:02:18.110
maybe we will I don't
know ask us anything,

00:02:18.110 --> 00:02:18.943
ask us anything.

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:21.700
But Kellye maybe a good place to start

00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:24.533
is what is the Law School
Admission's Council?

00:02:25.940 --> 00:02:30.400
- Well we love to basically help students

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:32.220
find their way to law school.

00:02:32.220 --> 00:02:34.410
We think of ourselves as an organization

00:02:34.410 --> 00:02:37.370
that helps advance equity in law

00:02:37.370 --> 00:02:40.940
and we help candidates find a good match

00:02:40.940 --> 00:02:42.780
when they're looking for a law school

00:02:42.780 --> 00:02:45.600
and make sure that we
provide the support they need

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:48.080
to continue their enrollment journey.

00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:49.820
You know Sal a lot of people

00:02:49.820 --> 00:02:51.690
don't have lawyers in their family.

00:02:51.690 --> 00:02:53.840
You know I'm a first
generation college grad,

00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:57.300
and law school grad, and so
we really try and reach out

00:02:57.300 --> 00:02:59.060
and provide a lot of information

00:02:59.060 --> 00:03:01.173
and build the pipeline for justice.

00:03:02.610 --> 00:03:05.460
- And most famously, the
Law School Admission's Test

00:03:05.460 --> 00:03:07.870
which actually I took, I was
pre-law for a period of time,

00:03:07.870 --> 00:03:10.050
maybe we'll go there.

00:03:10.050 --> 00:03:11.765
But I'm curious 'cause
you know one of the things

00:03:11.765 --> 00:03:13.420
that I look to do in these live streams

00:03:13.420 --> 00:03:14.950
is just understand how our guests

00:03:14.950 --> 00:03:17.930
ended up in the path they
are in and you mentioned

00:03:17.930 --> 00:03:21.006
you're a first generation
college graduate,

00:03:21.006 --> 00:03:23.180
what got you into the law profession?

00:03:23.180 --> 00:03:24.770
What was kind of your career arc

00:03:24.770 --> 00:03:27.230
from maybe thinking about going into law

00:03:27.230 --> 00:03:30.180
to now being the head of the
Law School Admission's Council?

00:03:30.180 --> 00:03:32.770
- Sure, well it's a little convoluted

00:03:32.770 --> 00:03:36.220
because I started my life out
wanting to be a quarterback

00:03:36.220 --> 00:03:38.980
on an NFL team, but my dad told me

00:03:38.980 --> 00:03:40.170
I was a little short for that

00:03:40.170 --> 00:03:42.720
so I should think up something else.

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:46.910
But in all seriousness I
was very avid into sports

00:03:46.910 --> 00:03:49.530
when I was in high school and in college

00:03:49.530 --> 00:03:53.960
and so it really wasn't until
I was a sports reporter,

00:03:53.960 --> 00:03:55.890
and so I majored in journalism,

00:03:55.890 --> 00:03:59.820
and then I found a class
called, Communications Law,

00:03:59.820 --> 00:04:02.380
that I just fell in love
with and from that minute on

00:04:02.380 --> 00:04:05.010
I knew I really wanted to
think about law school.

00:04:05.010 --> 00:04:08.830
So I had a little time
in-between undergrad and law

00:04:08.830 --> 00:04:11.130
when I worked in sales and marketing

00:04:11.130 --> 00:04:15.210
and then went to law school
and have been so happy I did.

00:04:15.210 --> 00:04:17.530
There are so many things
you can do with a law degree

00:04:17.530 --> 00:04:20.600
and I've really enjoyed
being in this profession.

00:04:20.600 --> 00:04:22.640
- And what was it about that
course in Communications Law

00:04:22.640 --> 00:04:23.983
that got you hooked?

00:04:25.170 --> 00:04:28.080
- You know we were asked
to go to the law library

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:30.370
and to find it on the campus and to go in

00:04:30.370 --> 00:04:35.040
and read a famous case that
was about what's called,

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:37.640
liable, which is when you
say something negative

00:04:37.640 --> 00:04:40.730
about a public figure and
could you be held responsible

00:04:40.730 --> 00:04:42.500
for damages for that.

00:04:42.500 --> 00:04:44.860
This case is called, "New
York Times versus Sullivan,"

00:04:44.860 --> 00:04:47.130
and it's a famous one and
we were supposed to find it

00:04:47.130 --> 00:04:48.300
and read it.

00:04:48.300 --> 00:04:50.250
And I don't know really still what it was

00:04:50.250 --> 00:04:52.050
but the minute I walked
in that law library

00:04:52.050 --> 00:04:54.460
and I started reading that case

00:04:54.460 --> 00:04:57.380
I realized the law's made up of stories,

00:04:57.380 --> 00:05:00.490
you know, everyone has
a story and these cases

00:05:00.490 --> 00:05:04.130
are the stories of times
when people may have had

00:05:04.130 --> 00:05:05.890
a difficult time in their life,

00:05:05.890 --> 00:05:10.180
or a challenging issue, and
so I loved reading their story

00:05:10.180 --> 00:05:14.240
and seeing how the law can
help people find a voice

00:05:14.240 --> 00:05:15.603
and find justice.

00:05:16.500 --> 00:05:19.090
- And then how did that
trajectory keep going?

00:05:19.090 --> 00:05:20.630
I actually felt similar things.

00:05:20.630 --> 00:05:22.760
I took a business law class
while in business school

00:05:22.760 --> 00:05:25.230
and I just was enamored by it

00:05:25.230 --> 00:05:28.500
and obviously I was pre-law
for a moment in undergrad too

00:05:28.500 --> 00:05:30.310
so I was definitely intrigued.

00:05:30.310 --> 00:05:32.670
I read that book, "One L," and
I was really intrigued by it

00:05:32.670 --> 00:05:36.950
but the, so how did you go from law school

00:05:36.950 --> 00:05:37.783
to where you are now?

00:05:37.783 --> 00:05:40.300
What was the, you know, your path?

00:05:40.300 --> 00:05:43.140
- Well and let me just say
I know you keep pretty busy

00:05:43.140 --> 00:05:45.630
and we love what you do,
but I can still talk to you

00:05:45.630 --> 00:05:47.100
about law school, you know.

00:05:47.970 --> 00:05:50.723
- My mom might like that,
law and medical school.

00:05:51.670 --> 00:05:52.870
- That's right, exactly.

00:05:54.040 --> 00:05:56.530
So I really enjoyed law school and I went

00:05:56.530 --> 00:05:59.050
to Indiana University
in Bloomington, Indiana

00:05:59.050 --> 00:06:01.820
which is where I'm from
and I was so fortunate

00:06:01.820 --> 00:06:05.010
to grow up in a town with
a big state university.

00:06:05.010 --> 00:06:07.410
And I went there undergrad and law school

00:06:07.410 --> 00:06:09.890
and after law school I had the honor

00:06:09.890 --> 00:06:12.470
to clerk for a federal judge

00:06:12.470 --> 00:06:14.240
and I learned a lot during that year

00:06:14.240 --> 00:06:17.500
about watching the judges hear the cases

00:06:17.500 --> 00:06:19.970
and the lawyers argue the cases.

00:06:19.970 --> 00:06:23.750
And I decided then that
I really was interested

00:06:23.750 --> 00:06:26.330
in being in the teaching side of law.

00:06:26.330 --> 00:06:29.550
There were things about
legal education that I liked,

00:06:29.550 --> 00:06:31.490
but also things I thought could be better

00:06:31.490 --> 00:06:33.680
and so I was interested
in being a law professor.

00:06:33.680 --> 00:06:38.290
So I started my career teaching law school

00:06:38.290 --> 00:06:40.460
and was a professor teaching classes

00:06:40.460 --> 00:06:42.170
in the business law area,

00:06:42.170 --> 00:06:44.440
and also in the law and inequality area.

00:06:44.440 --> 00:06:47.060
Things like race and law, gender and law,

00:06:47.060 --> 00:06:51.090
law and sexuality, and
really enjoyed that.

00:06:51.090 --> 00:06:53.310
After a few years I was asked to serve

00:06:53.310 --> 00:06:56.460
as the dean of the law
school where I was teaching

00:06:56.460 --> 00:06:58.430
and I did that for about five years

00:06:58.430 --> 00:06:59.280
and then I became

00:06:59.280 --> 00:07:02.380
the dean of the University
of Washington's School of Law

00:07:02.380 --> 00:07:05.320
for eight years prior to coming to

00:07:05.320 --> 00:07:07.480
the Law School Admission Council.

00:07:07.480 --> 00:07:08.900
And I was really intrigued by

00:07:08.900 --> 00:07:10.300
the Law School Admission Council

00:07:10.300 --> 00:07:13.780
'cause I had always tried to
work for educational equity.

00:07:13.780 --> 00:07:16.750
I believe so strongly in
the power of education

00:07:16.750 --> 00:07:19.880
and so this is a place
where we can really help

00:07:19.880 --> 00:07:22.820
even the playing field and help candidates

00:07:22.820 --> 00:07:24.593
advance their educations.

00:07:25.620 --> 00:07:27.120
- Yeah no and I definitely
wanna get into that

00:07:27.120 --> 00:07:28.970
talking about, we're already
getting a lot of questions

00:07:28.970 --> 00:07:31.700
on YouTube about the LSAT and COVID,

00:07:31.700 --> 00:07:34.180
but before we get to all of that

00:07:34.180 --> 00:07:35.430
I'd love your thoughts on

00:07:36.440 --> 00:07:37.730
what are some of the misconceptions

00:07:37.730 --> 00:07:39.920
about the law career and stereotypes,

00:07:39.920 --> 00:07:41.160
I guess what are accurate stereotypes

00:07:41.160 --> 00:07:43.400
and what are less then
accurate stereotypes?

00:07:43.400 --> 00:07:46.960
- (laughing) Well I think
one of the first things

00:07:46.960 --> 00:07:49.890
I would say about that is that many times

00:07:49.890 --> 00:07:52.350
when people think about what is a lawyer,

00:07:52.350 --> 00:07:55.053
what comes to mind first
is perhaps a judge,

00:07:56.220 --> 00:07:59.020
or a lawyer who is in court,

00:07:59.020 --> 00:08:01.030
and that is part of what lawyers do,

00:08:01.030 --> 00:08:03.590
but lawyers are also, I like to say,

00:08:03.590 --> 00:08:06.630
you know, problem solvers in every field,

00:08:06.630 --> 00:08:09.300
and they're also in many
ways referred to as sometimes

00:08:09.300 --> 00:08:12.090
as engineers for justice.

00:08:12.090 --> 00:08:14.530
You know the law is what gives our world

00:08:14.530 --> 00:08:19.220
the possibility to thrive
by enforcing contracts,

00:08:19.220 --> 00:08:22.110
and making sure people
keep their commitments,

00:08:22.110 --> 00:08:26.880
so really lawyers work in every
single field that there is.

00:08:26.880 --> 00:08:28.680
And you know I like to say

00:08:28.680 --> 00:08:31.590
that if you think of a
university, or the world,

00:08:31.590 --> 00:08:33.550
law's kind of at the middle, the hub,

00:08:33.550 --> 00:08:36.260
every other thing has a connection to it.

00:08:36.260 --> 00:08:39.110
And so no matter what your
passion, if it's environment,

00:08:39.110 --> 00:08:43.320
or civil rights, or music,
or representing athletes,

00:08:43.320 --> 00:08:47.550
or arguing in court, or
building businesses up,

00:08:47.550 --> 00:08:50.430
and helping technology firms thrive,

00:08:50.430 --> 00:08:52.773
lawyers do all those things and more.

00:08:53.880 --> 00:08:57.150
- I like that phrase you mentioned,

00:08:57.150 --> 00:08:58.850
engineers for justice.

00:08:58.850 --> 00:09:00.540
You know I remember being surprised

00:09:00.540 --> 00:09:02.087
when I was an undergraduate and I said,

00:09:02.087 --> 00:09:03.770
"maybe I'll go into law,"

00:09:03.770 --> 00:09:07.077
and I remember I started taking
practice tests for the LSAT

00:09:07.077 --> 00:09:10.057
and I was like, "this is like
a computer science exam."

00:09:13.108 --> 00:09:14.600
You know it has these
deductive reasoning problems,

00:09:14.600 --> 00:09:16.040
these games, and obviously

00:09:16.040 --> 00:09:17.890
some reading comprehension and logic.

00:09:18.990 --> 00:09:22.190
Why do you think that's, why
do you think that's the core

00:09:22.190 --> 00:09:25.550
of the LSAT, this like logic,
this deductive reasoning,

00:09:25.550 --> 00:09:27.760
et cetera, I mean it appealed to me

00:09:27.760 --> 00:09:29.560
as someone who had a very strong math

00:09:29.560 --> 00:09:31.220
and engineering background--
- Yeah.

00:09:31.220 --> 00:09:33.960
- And most people don't
see that connection.

00:09:33.960 --> 00:09:36.270
- No, it's true, but the math majors

00:09:36.270 --> 00:09:37.783
do really well in law school
and so do music majors

00:09:39.736 --> 00:09:41.510
and so do many majors.

00:09:41.510 --> 00:09:43.683
I mean one of the fun
things about law school

00:09:43.683 --> 00:09:47.790
is that it takes people from every major

00:09:47.790 --> 00:09:50.590
and all of them can drive.

00:09:50.590 --> 00:09:53.620
So I think really, you
know, the LSAT is designed

00:09:53.620 --> 00:09:56.750
to test the basic skills that it takes

00:09:56.750 --> 00:09:58.690
to be successful in law school

00:09:58.690 --> 00:10:02.110
and so it's not just a test
it's a way to build your skill

00:10:02.110 --> 00:10:04.700
that you're gonna need when you get there.

00:10:04.700 --> 00:10:08.400
And we've loved having the free test prep

00:10:08.400 --> 00:10:11.070
through the Khan Academy because
we know that really helps

00:10:11.070 --> 00:10:12.650
that skill building.

00:10:12.650 --> 00:10:15.080
But Sal lawyers are, in many ways,

00:10:15.080 --> 00:10:17.000
they're creative problem solvers.

00:10:17.000 --> 00:10:20.080
They have to learn to
think really creatively

00:10:20.080 --> 00:10:22.980
and to be able to, what I sometimes call,

00:10:22.980 --> 00:10:24.360
look around corners.

00:10:24.360 --> 00:10:28.430
In other words, what comes
next, what's gonna happen next,

00:10:28.430 --> 00:10:30.330
how do you navigate uncertainty?

00:10:30.330 --> 00:10:33.610
And so the skills that the LSAT test

00:10:33.610 --> 00:10:36.090
are exactly the ones that one needs

00:10:36.090 --> 00:10:38.390
to be able to do well as a lawyer

00:10:38.390 --> 00:10:41.350
and those are things like
reading really critically

00:10:41.350 --> 00:10:43.713
and understanding what you're reading,

00:10:44.730 --> 00:10:47.740
thinking with reason and logic,

00:10:47.740 --> 00:10:50.170
being able to reason from evidence,

00:10:50.170 --> 00:10:53.300
and so those are the skills
that'll really help you be

00:10:53.300 --> 00:10:55.880
of great use to your clients

00:10:55.880 --> 00:10:58.823
because you'll be able to help
them solve their problems.

00:10:59.670 --> 00:11:00.870
- No and I really like your point.

00:11:00.870 --> 00:11:02.720
You know I think all too often

00:11:02.720 --> 00:11:04.300
we either perceive ourselves,

00:11:04.300 --> 00:11:07.350
or we perceive other people
as oh, they're math people,

00:11:07.350 --> 00:11:10.423
they're science people, they're, you know,

00:11:11.810 --> 00:11:13.370
they're eloquent their law people,

00:11:13.370 --> 00:11:14.800
that's kind of one of the stereotypes,

00:11:14.800 --> 00:11:17.610
or they're a good writers,
whatever it might be,

00:11:17.610 --> 00:11:19.480
but the reality is, I always like

00:11:19.480 --> 00:11:21.090
to give the example of Abraham Lincoln.

00:11:21.090 --> 00:11:24.000
Abraham Lincoln obviously practiced law

00:11:24.000 --> 00:11:26.870
but he thought to make
himself a better lawyer

00:11:26.870 --> 00:11:30.180
he actually learned all
of the geometry proofs

00:11:30.180 --> 00:11:32.280
in Euclid's elements.

00:11:32.280 --> 00:11:35.010
And actually, James
Garfield, another lawyer,

00:11:35.010 --> 00:11:37.230
he actually has a novel,
"Proof of Pythagorean Theorem,"

00:11:37.230 --> 00:11:38.530
which he did while sitting

00:11:38.530 --> 00:11:40.150
in the U.S. House of Representatives

00:11:40.150 --> 00:11:43.440
so there's actually more cross-connections

00:11:43.440 --> 00:11:45.710
then I think most people realize.

00:11:45.710 --> 00:11:48.790
You know talking about our
relationship with Khan Academy,

00:11:48.790 --> 00:11:50.930
the LSAT, and we'll talk more about that,

00:11:50.930 --> 00:11:53.887
but you know one question from
YouTube, Mark Murray asked,

00:11:53.887 --> 00:11:57.110
"how can we increase diversity
in the legal profession?"

00:11:57.110 --> 00:11:59.040
So I'd love your take on that generally

00:11:59.040 --> 00:12:00.920
and then I'd also love your take on how

00:12:00.920 --> 00:12:04.300
hopefully our partnership
might play a role there?

00:12:04.300 --> 00:12:06.810
- Sure, well diversity and equity

00:12:06.810 --> 00:12:09.980
are so critical for law
because our legal profession

00:12:09.980 --> 00:12:12.530
really needs to mirror society

00:12:12.530 --> 00:12:15.720
so that people trust it and
so that we can really see

00:12:15.720 --> 00:12:19.010
all the issues that
arise in a diverse world.

00:12:19.010 --> 00:12:21.620
So the Law School Admission Test

00:12:21.620 --> 00:12:23.890
and the Law School Admission Council,

00:12:23.890 --> 00:12:27.100
our whole origination
story is around equity.

00:12:27.100 --> 00:12:30.400
We were formed to help
even the playing field

00:12:30.400 --> 00:12:32.910
so that it didn't depend
just on who you knew,

00:12:32.910 --> 00:12:34.800
or which school you went to,

00:12:34.800 --> 00:12:37.770
you could actually show
the skills you have

00:12:37.770 --> 00:12:41.820
and be able to get attention
for what you deserve,

00:12:41.820 --> 00:12:44.930
which is how you think
about complex problems.

00:12:44.930 --> 00:12:49.930
So we really prioritize building
the pipeline to law school

00:12:50.310 --> 00:12:53.580
because as I noted it's not always clear

00:12:53.580 --> 00:12:57.130
that some people know
how to go to law school,

00:12:57.130 --> 00:12:58.690
some people know that pathway,

00:12:58.690 --> 00:13:01.980
some people have good
mentors, others may not.

00:13:01.980 --> 00:13:04.980
And so we really try
and build the pipeline

00:13:04.980 --> 00:13:08.247
and open that window up
so we can say, you know,

00:13:08.247 --> 00:13:10.440
"we need your voice in law too."

00:13:10.440 --> 00:13:13.220
And the more diverse those voices in law,

00:13:13.220 --> 00:13:16.460
the more law will get closer

00:13:16.460 --> 00:13:18.880
to that quest for equal justice

00:13:18.880 --> 00:13:20.590
and that's what we all strive for

00:13:20.590 --> 00:13:22.820
is to really merge law and justice

00:13:22.820 --> 00:13:25.510
and so we know that diversity and equity

00:13:25.510 --> 00:13:26.963
are critical parts of that.

00:13:28.040 --> 00:13:31.867
- And a related question
from YouTube, Layeti asks,

00:13:31.867 --> 00:13:35.610
"how does the LSAC support and encourage

00:13:35.610 --> 00:13:36.880
first generation low-income students

00:13:36.880 --> 00:13:37.720
to apply for law school,"

00:13:37.720 --> 00:13:39.140
and I'll just add to that, you know,

00:13:39.140 --> 00:13:40.240
you said you said you
were a first generation

00:13:40.240 --> 00:13:42.060
college student and law student,

00:13:42.060 --> 00:13:44.420
what would you tell to students out there

00:13:44.420 --> 00:13:46.930
who are thinking, "well
it's kind of intriguing,

00:13:46.930 --> 00:13:49.360
it seems like a great
profession, but it's,

00:13:49.360 --> 00:13:52.620
you know after undergrad
it's three more years,

00:13:52.620 --> 00:13:55.070
I'm probably gonna have to incur debt,

00:13:55.070 --> 00:13:57.047
tuition doesn't seem cheap,"

00:13:58.010 --> 00:14:00.810
what would you tell them if
they're thinking about it?

00:14:00.810 --> 00:14:02.730
- You know I think the
most important thing

00:14:02.730 --> 00:14:05.593
I would tell them is we really need you.

00:14:06.600 --> 00:14:08.490
Coming out of the COVID crisis

00:14:08.490 --> 00:14:11.570
and with all of the
struggles our world has

00:14:11.570 --> 00:14:13.670
for equality and for justice,

00:14:13.670 --> 00:14:16.610
we really need voices like
yours in the legal profession.

00:14:16.610 --> 00:14:20.590
So I want you to know that
the students who do go,

00:14:20.590 --> 00:14:22.250
it is an important investment,

00:14:22.250 --> 00:14:23.950
but they're really glad they did.

00:14:23.950 --> 00:14:27.390
The careers are very
diverse and fulfilling

00:14:27.390 --> 00:14:31.550
and we do a lot to help you
on that enrollment journey.

00:14:31.550 --> 00:14:35.660
We have the free LSAT prep
through the Khan Academy,

00:14:35.660 --> 00:14:39.940
also at LSAC.org we have
a lot of resources there

00:14:39.940 --> 00:14:44.940
to help candidates understand
how to navigate that pathway

00:14:45.700 --> 00:14:48.060
and then we work with
our member law schools

00:14:48.060 --> 00:14:49.830
and they all do a lot of outreach

00:14:49.830 --> 00:14:52.180
and what we call pipeline building too

00:14:52.180 --> 00:14:55.220
to help introduce
students to what it means

00:14:55.220 --> 00:14:58.230
to go to law school and
how to do that well.

00:14:58.230 --> 00:15:02.720
And I think that one of the
things is just so critical

00:15:02.720 --> 00:15:06.620
is that lawyers are
really a wonderful group

00:15:06.620 --> 00:15:10.700
about mentoring new students

00:15:10.700 --> 00:15:15.180
and so we love to share
our actual thoughts

00:15:15.180 --> 00:15:17.520
about what it means to be in law

00:15:17.520 --> 00:15:20.960
because some people I think
have too narrow a view

00:15:20.960 --> 00:15:23.040
of what it means to be a lawyer.

00:15:23.040 --> 00:15:25.580
One of the best examples
I like to give of that Sal

00:15:25.580 --> 00:15:29.690
is that I'll often hear
someone say, "I have three kids

00:15:29.690 --> 00:15:32.680
and one of them they just
argue with me all the time,"

00:15:32.680 --> 00:15:36.130
so that, I told them,
"hey go to law school."

00:15:36.130 --> 00:15:38.500
Well what I wanna say is yes that person

00:15:38.500 --> 00:15:42.300
may wanna go to law school
and do well as an advocate

00:15:42.300 --> 00:15:44.520
but if one of those
children's a peace maker

00:15:44.520 --> 00:15:49.360
we need you too, law is a
peace-making profession as well.

00:15:49.360 --> 00:15:53.240
It builds a lot of common understanding,

00:15:53.240 --> 00:15:56.220
it builds agreement, negotiation,

00:15:56.220 --> 00:15:59.010
it finds ways to bring people together

00:15:59.010 --> 00:16:01.090
and move forward collectively.

00:16:01.090 --> 00:16:03.390
So whether you're the one who argues,

00:16:03.390 --> 00:16:06.223
or you're the peace maker,
there's room for you in law.

00:16:07.620 --> 00:16:09.000
- I'll just doubly underline that.

00:16:09.000 --> 00:16:10.810
We actually have great internal council

00:16:10.810 --> 00:16:12.860
here at Khan Academy,
her name's Julia Cowles,

00:16:12.860 --> 00:16:14.780
and what I appreciate about her

00:16:14.780 --> 00:16:17.610
is a lot of people have these stereotypes

00:16:17.610 --> 00:16:21.330
that lawyers are, you know
they're always risk adverse,

00:16:21.330 --> 00:16:23.610
or they're trying to stick
it to the other person,

00:16:23.610 --> 00:16:26.460
or they're trying to kind
of overwhelm everyone

00:16:26.460 --> 00:16:28.730
with really hard to understand terminology

00:16:28.730 --> 00:16:31.280
and so everyone feels a little bit lost.

00:16:31.280 --> 00:16:32.680
But the really great lawyers,

00:16:32.680 --> 00:16:35.320
and I would consider
Julia to be one of them,

00:16:35.320 --> 00:16:37.830
they know how to, your language

00:16:37.830 --> 00:16:39.600
has to be precise as a lawyer

00:16:39.600 --> 00:16:40.730
so that both parties--
- Right.

00:16:40.730 --> 00:16:43.437
- Don't later say, "hey, I thought this?"

00:16:43.437 --> 00:16:45.400
"No, no, no this is what we agreed to."

00:16:45.400 --> 00:16:47.120
So if the language is precise

00:16:47.120 --> 00:16:49.050
then everyone knows what's going to happen

00:16:49.050 --> 00:16:52.000
and you're less likely to get
into a disagreement later,

00:16:52.000 --> 00:16:54.460
but then also around the problem solving,

00:16:54.460 --> 00:16:56.490
around can you make that language precise

00:16:56.490 --> 00:16:58.220
but both parties don't think

00:16:58.220 --> 00:17:00.180
that they're on kind of
the losing end of the deal,

00:17:00.180 --> 00:17:02.600
and I think the best
lawyers are definitely

00:17:02.600 --> 00:17:05.730
those problem solvers and
we definitely have one here

00:17:05.730 --> 00:17:06.980
at Khan Academy.

00:17:06.980 --> 00:17:11.767
A question from Facebook,
Loraine Alaura asks,

00:17:11.767 --> 00:17:13.580
"would you consider
this a path for someone

00:17:13.580 --> 00:17:14.847
who's middle aged?"

00:17:15.920 --> 00:17:17.640
- You know I would.

00:17:17.640 --> 00:17:20.170
My oldest student ever that graduated

00:17:20.170 --> 00:17:23.000
was a doctor who graduated when he was 82

00:17:23.000 --> 00:17:26.200
and he decided he wanted
a law degree, yeah,

00:17:26.200 --> 00:17:27.970
I'll always remember him.
- Slight;y past middle age.

00:17:27.970 --> 00:17:29.910
- He was awesome, so no,

00:17:29.910 --> 00:17:32.510
it's true that many
people go to law school

00:17:32.510 --> 00:17:37.070
right after college, but I had
almost six years in-between

00:17:37.070 --> 00:17:39.270
undergrad and law school and many people

00:17:39.270 --> 00:17:41.230
have more years than that.

00:17:41.230 --> 00:17:43.850
And so it's a great profession

00:17:43.850 --> 00:17:47.910
and honestly when you've
worked in some other discipline

00:17:47.910 --> 00:17:50.610
many times you can then
use what you learned there

00:17:50.610 --> 00:17:53.700
and merge it with law and
enhance your career that way.

00:17:53.700 --> 00:17:57.560
So law school really does welcome people

00:17:57.560 --> 00:17:59.700
from very diverse backgrounds, you know,

00:17:59.700 --> 00:18:04.700
any major, any kind of, many
ages and the other thing

00:18:05.420 --> 00:18:07.530
that's so important to that I wanna say

00:18:07.530 --> 00:18:11.940
is that we also are
working with our schools

00:18:11.940 --> 00:18:15.600
and we and our schools
really are serious about it

00:18:15.600 --> 00:18:17.920
when we say we welcome diverse students,

00:18:17.920 --> 00:18:19.860
we mean that widely.

00:18:19.860 --> 00:18:22.930
View point, race and ethnic diversity,

00:18:22.930 --> 00:18:26.080
and also a key area today
is disability rights,

00:18:26.080 --> 00:18:28.430
and that's something that
we've been working really hard

00:18:28.430 --> 00:18:33.430
to open doors for opportunity
to more candidates

00:18:33.860 --> 00:18:35.870
who need accommodations in testing,

00:18:35.870 --> 00:18:39.663
or need accommodations in
pursuing their educations.

00:18:40.600 --> 00:18:43.680
- Yep, and from YouTube just
going into this COVID crisis,

00:18:43.680 --> 00:18:46.470
I'm curious generally how you're coping

00:18:46.470 --> 00:18:49.630
and how the whole
organization, legal profession,

00:18:49.630 --> 00:18:51.917
but Lainie asks specifically,

00:18:52.771 --> 00:18:55.380
"how is LSAC administering the LSAT

00:18:55.380 --> 00:18:59.370
and the law school
application during COVID-19?"

00:18:59.370 --> 00:19:01.100
- Yeah that's a great question

00:19:01.100 --> 00:19:04.410
and we had to move very quickly

00:19:04.410 --> 00:19:09.200
to take the law school
admission test into a format

00:19:09.200 --> 00:19:11.600
that could be administered remotely.

00:19:11.600 --> 00:19:15.180
So once we realized that COVID

00:19:15.180 --> 00:19:20.000
was going to restrict travel
and gatherings for some time

00:19:20.000 --> 00:19:21.870
we couldn't give the test in the field

00:19:21.870 --> 00:19:26.350
so our developers and
personnel worked day and night

00:19:26.350 --> 00:19:30.120
to convert the LSAT to a system

00:19:30.120 --> 00:19:32.970
that can be delivered and
you can take it from home

00:19:32.970 --> 00:19:34.670
with remote proctoring.

00:19:34.670 --> 00:19:37.760
And that's begun and
that way we can make sure

00:19:37.760 --> 00:19:40.460
the candidates continue
their enrollment journey

00:19:40.460 --> 00:19:42.780
so it's really important
for us to be able to do that

00:19:42.780 --> 00:19:44.730
and we're gonna do that
as long as we need to

00:19:44.730 --> 00:19:46.880
while this crisis is on.

00:19:46.880 --> 00:19:50.180
The other thing we've done
is that we've been horrified

00:19:50.180 --> 00:19:54.360
by how much this crisis
has affected the world

00:19:54.360 --> 00:19:57.330
in different ways, based
on again, inequality,

00:19:57.330 --> 00:20:00.010
and so we've really been
redoubling our efforts

00:20:00.010 --> 00:20:02.060
on diversity and equity.

00:20:02.060 --> 00:20:04.690
We've been doing a lot of
webinars for candidates

00:20:04.690 --> 00:20:06.100
and also for our schools

00:20:06.100 --> 00:20:09.260
to try and support
everyone through this time,

00:20:09.260 --> 00:20:12.870
but that's a really good question
and what might have taken

00:20:12.870 --> 00:20:16.110
two years to do we were able
to really do very quickly

00:20:16.110 --> 00:20:17.110
'cause we needed to.

00:20:17.110 --> 00:20:19.690
We had the help and I was really proud

00:20:19.690 --> 00:20:21.583
of how LSAC responded.

00:20:22.880 --> 00:20:24.310
- And just following up on that,

00:20:24.310 --> 00:20:26.770
what are trends, or are there trends

00:20:26.770 --> 00:20:31.010
that you think are going to
happen because of COVID-19?

00:20:31.010 --> 00:20:32.920
Do you think applications will go up,

00:20:32.920 --> 00:20:35.530
they'll stay the same,
do you think more people

00:20:35.530 --> 00:20:38.070
are thinking about graduate school?

00:20:38.070 --> 00:20:40.790
- Yeah, I think that we're gonna see

00:20:40.790 --> 00:20:42.630
strong demand for education.

00:20:42.630 --> 00:20:45.070
I mean obviously there's a
lot of uncertainty right now

00:20:45.070 --> 00:20:47.330
about what will the fall look like

00:20:47.330 --> 00:20:50.720
and will it be, will people
be back to campus or not,

00:20:50.720 --> 00:20:54.040
will it still be that
everyone will need to be

00:20:54.040 --> 00:20:56.280
learning remotely for awhile?

00:20:56.280 --> 00:20:59.160
But many students are familiar with that,

00:20:59.160 --> 00:21:00.400
they're comfortable with it

00:21:00.400 --> 00:21:04.960
and so we're seeing still
very, very strong demand

00:21:04.960 --> 00:21:08.280
for legal education for this coming fall

00:21:08.280 --> 00:21:10.860
and then the cycle where
people start to think about

00:21:10.860 --> 00:21:13.550
applying for next fall's
beginning as well,

00:21:13.550 --> 00:21:15.430
and again, it's still very strong.

00:21:15.430 --> 00:21:18.040
And I think that one of the areas

00:21:18.040 --> 00:21:20.300
that I've always encouraged
students to think about

00:21:20.300 --> 00:21:24.610
is in timing, when do I go to law school,

00:21:24.610 --> 00:21:27.600
is always think about,
well what's my option,

00:21:27.600 --> 00:21:31.390
and right now the economy
is fairly disrupted,

00:21:31.390 --> 00:21:33.680
you know in many areas severely,

00:21:33.680 --> 00:21:37.780
and so I think that in a time
when employment's difficult

00:21:37.780 --> 00:21:41.600
investing in education is
a really great solution.

00:21:41.600 --> 00:21:45.430
And so I'm hoping and anticipating

00:21:45.430 --> 00:21:48.310
that we'll still see a very strong demand

00:21:48.310 --> 00:21:51.920
for legal ed and I'm glad
of that because we need it.

00:21:51.920 --> 00:21:55.090
This crisis has really shown so many areas

00:21:55.090 --> 00:21:58.020
that lawyers are gonna
have to help rebuild

00:21:58.020 --> 00:22:02.350
and the rule of law needs to be respected

00:22:02.350 --> 00:22:05.750
and strong and the devastation

00:22:05.750 --> 00:22:07.530
that's been wrought by this pandemic,

00:22:07.530 --> 00:22:10.290
and continues to be, is something lawyers

00:22:10.290 --> 00:22:13.810
are gonna have to help
with and help repair.

00:22:13.810 --> 00:22:16.040
- Yeah, and how do you see,

00:22:16.040 --> 00:22:17.820
what are law schools
talking about right now

00:22:17.820 --> 00:22:19.470
in terms of campus openings,

00:22:19.470 --> 00:22:21.150
are they just following whatever their,

00:22:21.150 --> 00:22:23.100
the university they're a part of doing?

00:22:24.170 --> 00:22:26.390
Can you have an effective legal education

00:22:26.390 --> 00:22:28.590
if the first semester, or the first year

00:22:28.590 --> 00:22:30.953
is done over a video
conference of some kind?

00:22:31.870 --> 00:22:33.970
- You know it's a hard question

00:22:33.970 --> 00:22:38.040
because legal education is a
personalized form of education.

00:22:38.040 --> 00:22:40.090
We don't do a lot of just lecturing,

00:22:40.090 --> 00:22:43.720
we really engage with students
and have a lot of discussion,

00:22:43.720 --> 00:22:47.730
we do a lot of hands-on
experiential education

00:22:47.730 --> 00:22:49.890
where you're actually
working with real clients

00:22:49.890 --> 00:22:52.480
and solving real problems.

00:22:52.480 --> 00:22:56.330
So it has taken the
creativity of law schools

00:22:56.330 --> 00:22:58.700
and they've responded in amazing ways

00:22:58.700 --> 00:23:01.910
and had to respond as
we all did so quickly.

00:23:01.910 --> 00:23:05.500
So I know this year I was
teaching law and leadership

00:23:05.500 --> 00:23:09.430
at Villanova and so one day
I walked out of my classroom

00:23:09.430 --> 00:23:12.640
not realizing I wouldn't
be able to walk back in

00:23:12.640 --> 00:23:15.100
and we quickly took the course online.

00:23:15.100 --> 00:23:18.240
Well that's being done
all across the world

00:23:18.240 --> 00:23:21.220
and I think that as the fall comes,

00:23:21.220 --> 00:23:23.440
since the schools have had even more time

00:23:23.440 --> 00:23:25.530
to prepare for that, we'll see

00:23:25.530 --> 00:23:28.040
an even stronger version of that

00:23:28.040 --> 00:23:32.180
if it's not possible to bring
the students back to campus.

00:23:32.180 --> 00:23:34.450
And right now I wish I
had an answer to that,

00:23:34.450 --> 00:23:36.230
and everyone does.

00:23:36.230 --> 00:23:40.530
I think that everyone is
preparing to try and bring

00:23:42.580 --> 00:23:46.110
some form of education back to campus,

00:23:46.110 --> 00:23:48.870
but that's something that
the schools and universities

00:23:48.870 --> 00:23:52.020
are working closely with
government officials on too

00:23:52.020 --> 00:23:53.800
to make sure it's safe to do so

00:23:53.800 --> 00:23:57.460
because obviously the safety
of students is paramount.

00:23:57.460 --> 00:23:59.360
So I think that there's a lot

00:23:59.360 --> 00:24:01.230
of contingency planning happening,

00:24:01.230 --> 00:24:03.510
some of you know, maybe some in person,

00:24:03.510 --> 00:24:06.460
some online, what if it has to stay online

00:24:06.460 --> 00:24:08.110
for a certain amount of time.

00:24:08.110 --> 00:24:09.950
But I really feel a lot of confidence

00:24:09.950 --> 00:24:13.130
in the enormous work the schools are doing

00:24:13.130 --> 00:24:15.930
to prepare for that and
they care about the students

00:24:15.930 --> 00:24:18.280
and they'll put them
first and do well by them.

00:24:19.160 --> 00:24:22.140
- Yeah, no that's super
helpful and I don't think

00:24:22.140 --> 00:24:23.950
anyone really knows how this--
- No.

00:24:23.950 --> 00:24:24.850
- Next year's gonna play out,

00:24:24.850 --> 00:24:28.810
but I think everyone's doing
pretty heroic jobs I mean.

00:24:28.810 --> 00:24:30.410
A somewhat random question from YouTube,

00:24:30.410 --> 00:24:33.877
Maha Kerkowitz, it says, "do
you know Ruth Bader Ginsburg?"

00:24:33.877 --> 00:24:36.650
- (laughing) I certainly
know of her and I've met her

00:24:36.650 --> 00:24:39.380
and had the great pleasure
of hearing her speak

00:24:39.380 --> 00:24:44.380
a number of times and what a
wonderful figure for our world

00:24:44.520 --> 00:24:46.820
and I admire her so very much,

00:24:46.820 --> 00:24:48.970
not only her work as a Justice,

00:24:48.970 --> 00:24:51.870
but the work she did
as a lawyer before that

00:24:51.870 --> 00:24:55.830
as an advocate for women and the law

00:24:55.830 --> 00:24:59.220
and an advocate on gender
equity more broadly.

00:24:59.220 --> 00:25:03.260
So she's one of the real
heroes of justice I think

00:25:03.260 --> 00:25:05.013
for many of us today.

00:25:06.060 --> 00:25:07.710
- Yeah, just as a final question,

00:25:07.710 --> 00:25:09.750
I'll just kind of group a
bunch of these questions.

00:25:09.750 --> 00:25:10.797
You know there's some questions like,

00:25:10.797 --> 00:25:13.150
"is law school hard," "or
how can I get through it,"

00:25:13.150 --> 00:25:14.410
you know I think there's these,

00:25:14.410 --> 00:25:16.890
you see in the movies these
people piling over these

00:25:16.890 --> 00:25:20.570
seemingly dense books and they
imagine that's law school.

00:25:20.570 --> 00:25:22.347
Someone who's an engineer,
Pria B.'s talking about,

00:25:22.347 --> 00:25:24.640
"would I make a good lawyer?"

00:25:24.640 --> 00:25:27.090
So you know just maybe
parting thoughts on anyone

00:25:27.090 --> 00:25:28.600
who's like, "well law seems interesting,

00:25:28.600 --> 00:25:32.180
but it seems hard,"
whatever that might mean.

00:25:32.180 --> 00:25:35.010
What would you tell them,
what advice would you give,

00:25:35.010 --> 00:25:37.310
or just life advice generally?

00:25:37.310 --> 00:25:40.370
- Yeah, so I guess in
terms of law school itself

00:25:40.370 --> 00:25:42.730
let me say that when I started law school

00:25:42.730 --> 00:25:44.777
I had no idea what to expect

00:25:44.777 --> 00:25:47.470
and I was worried it would be so hard

00:25:47.470 --> 00:25:51.360
and something that could be out of reach,

00:25:51.360 --> 00:25:54.800
but I wanna say that it is
really supportive today.

00:25:54.800 --> 00:25:56.910
Legal education has changed a lot

00:25:56.910 --> 00:26:00.670
from the "One L" book or
"The Paper Chase" movie

00:26:00.670 --> 00:26:03.350
you might have seen
and the faculty members

00:26:03.350 --> 00:26:06.370
are so supportive and
there's great infrastructure

00:26:06.370 --> 00:26:08.930
for student support, and academic support,

00:26:08.930 --> 00:26:11.080
career support, you name it.

00:26:11.080 --> 00:26:14.790
So I really know that today
law students can thrive

00:26:14.790 --> 00:26:17.080
and they get the support that they need.

00:26:17.080 --> 00:26:19.370
Now that's not to say that it isn't

00:26:19.370 --> 00:26:21.620
a rigorous form of education.

00:26:21.620 --> 00:26:24.350
Much of it's value is that it is

00:26:24.350 --> 00:26:27.230
and you will learn to
think hard in law school

00:26:27.230 --> 00:26:30.090
and you will learn to really
contribute to the world

00:26:30.090 --> 00:26:33.950
in so many ways and to really help people.

00:26:33.950 --> 00:26:36.930
I think it's hard when
you get a law degree

00:26:36.930 --> 00:26:39.390
and you get your license
you now have a license

00:26:39.390 --> 00:26:42.300
to help people, to make
change in the world,

00:26:42.300 --> 00:26:45.380
to do good in the world,
and that's something

00:26:45.380 --> 00:26:47.540
that I really think most of us

00:26:47.540 --> 00:26:49.900
really wanna find an avenue for.

00:26:49.900 --> 00:26:52.470
We see the ways that our world,

00:26:52.470 --> 00:26:54.270
as beautiful it is in some ways,

00:26:54.270 --> 00:26:58.260
is broken in many others
and we need to repair that.

00:26:58.260 --> 00:27:01.760
We need to work to make sure that everyone

00:27:01.760 --> 00:27:04.140
has a chance to thrive in this world

00:27:04.140 --> 00:27:05.930
and that's really what law does

00:27:05.930 --> 00:27:08.750
it tries to make sure that everyone

00:27:08.750 --> 00:27:10.520
has a chance to flourish.

00:27:10.520 --> 00:27:12.480
So I think if you have a passion

00:27:12.480 --> 00:27:14.520
for wanting to make the
world a better place

00:27:14.520 --> 00:27:16.920
there's really no better
investment you can make

00:27:16.920 --> 00:27:21.090
then learning the law and
following your passion

00:27:21.090 --> 00:27:23.167
in whatever direction that takes you.

00:27:24.397 --> 00:27:26.600
- You know Kellye you've inspired me

00:27:27.590 --> 00:27:30.650
and so thank you, thank you so
much for being a part of this

00:27:30.650 --> 00:27:32.460
I think you've just inspired a good chunk

00:27:32.460 --> 00:27:34.830
of new future lawyers who
can hopefully be the type

00:27:34.830 --> 00:27:37.870
that can bring justice
to the world for us all.

00:27:37.870 --> 00:27:41.100
- Well I think they can and
I wanna say thanks to you,

00:27:41.100 --> 00:27:42.820
you know you and the Khan Academy

00:27:42.820 --> 00:27:44.180
that you inspire all of us

00:27:44.180 --> 00:27:47.350
and educational equity is so critical

00:27:47.350 --> 00:27:48.970
and so thanks for all you do

00:27:48.970 --> 00:27:52.540
to make that so broadly available.

00:27:52.540 --> 00:27:54.170
We really love our partnership with you

00:27:54.170 --> 00:27:56.390
and look forward to it continuing.

